erified": true, "verification_score": 0.9, "verification_notes": "Netflix's Monster season 4 is 'The Lizzie Borden Story', following Dahmer, Menendez and Gein seasons; Borden was tried in 1893 for the axe murders of her father and stepmother."}, "created_at": null}, "cost_cents": null, "media_url": null, "image_subtype": null, "media_duration_seconds": null, "explanation": "Lizzie Borden was acquitted, but a skipping rhyme about giving her mother 'forty whacks' made her infamous for good. Ella Beatty plays her in the Netflix season.", "embedding_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "embedding_dim": 1536, "subtopic": null, "answer_key": null, "embedding_qa_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "expires_at": null, "freshness_tag": null, "boost_until": "2026-12-30T00:00:00Z"}, {"id": "e33477f5-99c3-403c-9812-e971cb21dba1", "question": "In Squid Game, the giant doll chants a Korean children's phrase before turning round. It literally says that a certain flower has bloomed. Which flower, South Korea's national flower?", "type": "text", "possible_answers": null, "correct_answer": "Hibiscus", "alternative_answers": ["Rose of Sharon", "Mugunghwa", "Hibiscus syriacus"], "headline_answer": null, "topic": "Streaming", "category": "entertainment", "difficulty": "hard", "tags": [], "language_dependent": false, "age_appropriate": "adults", "language": "en", "pack_id": null, "prompt_seed": "af636849077fb5c0", "created_at": "2026-10-14T13:05:08.696845", "created_by": null, "source": "generated", "source_url": "https://www.nationalworld.com/arts-and-culture/film-and-tv/mugunghwa-flower-has-bloomed-korean-experts-break-down-true-meaning-of-popular-squid-game-phrase-5209233", "source_excerpt": "Mugunghwa flower is Korea's national flower; in English, it's known as the 'Rose of Sharon'.", "usage_count": 0, "user_ratings": {}, "review_status": "approved", "reviewed_by": "machine:gates-v1", "reviewed_at": "2026-10-14T11:09:26.865883Z", "review_notes": null, "quality_ratings": null, "generation_metadata": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "provider": "anthropic", "prompt_version": "direct_v1", "pipeline": null, "generation_flow": "fun-redesign-72", "generation_temperature": null, "critique_model": null, "critique_score": null, "reasoning_pattern": "hidden_meaning", "fact_ids": [], "extra": {"stage": "initial_generation", "escape_hatch": true, "gen_craft_guards": true, "why_interesting": "Millions of viewers heard the creepy chant without knowing it is about a flower. It turns out to name the national symbol of the country the show comes from.", "topicality": {"tier": "none", "event_date": null, "rationale": "General Squid Game and Korean culture trivia, not tied to fresh news."}, "factcheck_tier": "web", "verified": true, "verification_score": 0.7, "verification_notes": "The chant refers to the mugunghwa (Hibiscus syriacus, rose of Sharon), South Korea's national flower, so 'Hibiscus' is acceptable, though 'Rose of Sharon' is the more specific common name."}, "created_at": null}, "cost_cents": null, "media_url": null, "image_subtype": null, "media_duration_seconds": null, "explanation": "The chant means 'the mugunghwa has bloomed'. The mugunghwa is a hibiscus, also called the rose of Sharon, and it is South Korea's national flower.", "embedding_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "embedding_dim": 1536, "subtopic": null, "answer_key": null, "embedding_qa_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "expires_at": null, "freshness_tag": null, "boost_until": null}, {"id": "434b060a-00f6-49c8-a59e-a99200c32e3f", "question": "Tetris, the falling-blocks puzzle that later conquered the Game Boy, was first programmed in 1984 at a computer research centre in which capital city?", "type": "text", "possible_answers": null, "correct_answer": "Moscow", "alternative_answers": ["Moscow, USSR", "Moscow, Soviet Union"], "headline_answer": null, "topic": "Video games", "category": "entertainment", "difficulty": "medium", "tags": [], "language_dependent": false, "age_appropriate": "all", "language": "en", "pack_id": null, "prompt_seed": "af636849077fb5c0", "created_at": "2026-10-14T13:05:08.696858", "created_by": null, "source": "generated", "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetris", "source_excerpt": "Alexey Pajitnov, a Soviet software engineer, created Tetris in his spare time while working at the Dorodnitsyn Computing Center of the Academy of Sciences.", "usage_count": 0, "user_ratings": {}, "review_status": "approved", "reviewed_by": "machine:gates-v1", "reviewed_at": "2026-10-14T11:09:26.865883Z", "review_notes": null, "quality_ratings": null, "generation_metadata": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "provider": "anthropic", "prompt_version": "direct_v1", "pipeline": null, "generation_flow": "fun-redesign-72", "generation_temperature": null, "critique_model": null, "critique_score": null, "reasoning_pattern": "origin_surprise", "fact_ids": [], "extra": {"stage": "initial_generation", "escape_hatch": true, "gen_craft_guards": true, "why_interesting": "Players tend to assume the most famous puzzle game came from Japan or the US. It actually came out of a Soviet research lab during the Cold War.", "topicality": {"tier": "none", "event_date": null, "rationale": "Historical video game trivia."}, "factcheck_tier": "web", "verified": true, "verification_score": 0.9, "verification_notes": "Pajitnov created Tetris in June 1984 at the Dorodnitsyn Computing Centre of the Academy of Sciences in Moscow; some sources say 1985, but 1984 is the commonly cited date."}, "created_at": null}, "cost_cents": null, "media_url": null, "image_subtype": null, "media_duration_seconds": null, "explanation": "Tetris was born at the Soviet Academy of Sciences in Moscow. That is why so many versions play Russian folk tunes such as Korobeiniki.", "embedding_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "embedding_dim": 1536, "subtopic": null, "answer_key": null, "embedding_qa_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "expires_at": null, "freshness_tag": null, "boost_until": null}, {"id": "eff7fa61-c9fa-4889-844d-89ead660ad28", "question": "Queen frontman Freddie Mercury was born Farrokh Bulsara in 1946 on which island off the coast of East Africa?", "type": "text", "possible_answers": null, "correct_answer": "Zanzibar", "alternative_answers": ["Unguja"], "headline_answer": null, "topic": "Music", "category": "entertainment", "difficulty": "hard", "tags": [], "language_dependent": false, "age_appropriate": "all", "language": "en", "pack_id": null, "prompt_seed": "af636849077fb5c0", "created_at": "2026-10-14T13:05:08.696878", "created_by": null, "source": "generated", "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freddie_Mercury", "source_excerpt": "born in Stone Town in the British protectorate of Zanzibar (now part of Tanzania) on 5 September 1946", "usage_count": 0, "user_ratings": {}, "review_status": "approved", "reviewed_by": "machine:gates-v1", "reviewed_at": "2026-10-14T11:09:26.865883Z", "review_notes": null, "quality_ratings": null, "generation_metadata": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "provider": "anthropic", "prompt_version": "direct_v1", "pipeline": null, "generation_flow": "fun-redesign-72", "generation_temperature": null, "critique_model": null, "critique_score": null, "reasoning_pattern": "surprising_origin", "fact_ids": [], "extra": {"stage": "initial_generation", "escape_hatch": true, "gen_craft_guards": true, "why_interesting": "The ultimate British rock frontman was born on an East African spice island. 'Off East Africa' gives a real path to the answer.", "topicality": {"tier": "none", "event_date": null, "rationale": "Biographical music history."}, "factcheck_tier": "web", "verified": true, "verification_score": 0.9, "verification_notes": "Wikipedia confirms Farrokh Bulsara was born 5 September 1946 in Stone Town, Zanzibar, an island off East Africa; this is a fixed historical fact."}, "created_at": null}, "cost_cents": null, "media_url": null, "image_subtype": null, "media_duration_seconds": null, "explanation": "Mercury was born in Stone Town, Zanzibar, to Parsi parents. He spent much of his childhood in India before the family moved to England.", "embedding_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "embedding_dim": 1536, "subtopic": null, "answer_key": null, "embedding_qa_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "expires_at": null, "freshness_tag": null, "boost_until": null}, {"id": "d2b2080e-7702-4712-90bb-699f008fa769", "question": "Queen's legendary Live Aid set at London's Wembley Stadium, often called the greatest live rock performance ever, took place in which year?", "type": "text_multichoice", "possible_answers": {"a": "1975", "b": "1985", "c": "1995"}, "correct_answer": "1985", "alternative_answers": [], "headline_answer": null, "topic": "Music", "category": "entertainment", "difficulty": "medium", "tags": [], "language_dependent": false, "age_appropriate": "all", "language": "en", "pack_id": null, "prompt_seed": "af636849077fb5c0", "created_at": "2026-10-14T13:05:08.696891", "created_by": null, "source": "generated", "source_url": "https://www.queenconcerts.com/detail/live/672/queen-live-13-07-1985-wembley-stadium-london-uk-live-aid-festival.html", "source_excerpt": "Queen live at the Wembley Stadium, London, UK (Live Aid festival) [13.07.1985]", "usage_count": 0, "user_ratings": {}, "review_status": "approved", "reviewed_by": "machine:gates-v1", "reviewed_at": "2026-10-14T11:09:26.865883Z", "review_notes": null, "quality_ratings": null, "generation_metadata": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "provider": "anthropic", "prompt_version": "direct_v1", "pipeline": null, "generation_flow": "fun-redesign-72", "generation_temperature": null, "critique_model": null, "critique_score": null, "reasoning_pattern": "year_guess", "fact_ids": [], "extra": {"stage": "initial_generation", "escape_hatch": true, "gen_craft_guards": true, "why_interesting": "The set is so iconic, recreated beat for beat in Bohemian Rhapsody, that players can place it by era reasoning even without knowing the year.", "topicality": {"tier": "none", "event_date": null, "rationale": "Music history from 1985."}, "factcheck_tier": "web", "verified": true, "verification_score": 0.9, "verification_notes": "Queen's Live Aid set at Wembley took place on 13 July 1985, per https://www.queenconcerts.com/detail/live/672/queen-live-13-07-1985-wembley-stadium-london-uk-live-aid-festival.html; no newer event can change this."}, "created_at": null}, "cost_cents": null, "media_url": null, "image_subtype": null, "media_duration_seconds": null, "explanation": "It was 13 July 1985, in front of about 72,000 people at Wembley and a TV audience in the billions. The charity concert raised money for famine relief in Ethiopia.", "embedding_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "embedding_dim": 1536, "subtopic": null, "answer_key": null, "embedding_qa_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "expires_at": null, "freshness_tag": null, "boost_until": null}, {"id": "6172d7e0-1ac3-4909-ac61-1c83284049b3", "question": "In Breaking Bad, chemistry teacher Walter White takes a criminal alias borrowed from a famous German physicist. That physicist is best known for a principle named after what?", "type": "text", "possible_answers": null, "correct_answer": "Uncertainty", "alternative_answers": ["Uncertainty principle", "Heisenberg uncertainty principle", "Indeterminacy"], "headline_answer": null, "topic": "TV", "category": "entertainment", "difficulty": "medium", "tags": [], "language_dependent": false, "age_appropriate": "all", "language": "en", "pack_id": null, "prompt_seed": "af636849077fb5c0", "created_at": "2026-10-14T13:05:08.696924", "created_by": null, "source": "generated", "source_url": "https://looper.com/1254107/breaking-bad-why-heisenberg-walter-whites-moniker-why-mattered", "source_excerpt": "This is a nod to the famed quantum physicist Werner Heisenberg.", "usage_count": 0, "user_ratings": {}, "review_status": "approved", "reviewed_by": "machine:gates-v1", "reviewed_at": "2026-10-14T11:09:26.865883Z", "review_notes": null, "quality_ratings": null, "generation_metadata": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "provider": "anthropic", "prompt_version": "direct_v1", "pipeline": null, "generation_flow": "fun-redesign-72", "generation_temperature": null, "critique_model": null, "critique_score": null, "reasoning_pattern": "name_origin", "fact_ids": [], "extra": {"stage": "initial_generation", "escape_hatch": true, "gen_craft_guards": true, "why_interesting": "Fans know the alias but rarely think about the science behind it. The principle's name also fits a man whose identity nobody can pin down.", "topicality": {"tier": "none", "event_date": null, "rationale": "TV and science trivia that is not news."}, "factcheck_tier": "web", "verified": true, "verification_score": 0.9, "verification_notes": "Walter White's alias Heisenberg is a nod to physicist Werner Heisenberg, known for the uncertainty principle; no newer developments affect this (https://looper.com/1254107/breaking-bad-why-heisenberg-walter-whites-moniker-why-mattered)."}, "created_at": null}, "cost_cents": null, "media_url": null, "image_subtype": null, "media_duration_seconds": null, "explanation": "The alias is Heisenberg, after Werner Heisenberg's uncertainty principle. It suits a man whose true identity the police can never be sure of.", "embedding_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "embedding_dim": 1536, "subtopic": null, "answer_key": null, "embedding_qa_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "expires_at": null, "freshness_tag": null, "boost_until": null}, {"id": "ee1bdb4f-d9d2-4794-a662-6e1443eb920d", "question": "Pikachu's name joins two Japanese sound effects: 'pika' for an electric sparkle, and 'chu' for the squeak of which animal?", "type": "text", "possible_answers": null, "correct_answer": "A mouse", "alternative_answers": ["Mouse", "Mice", "A rodent"], "headline_answer": null, "topic": "Video games", "category": "entertainment", "difficulty": "easy", "tags": [], "language_dependent": false, "age_appropriate": "all", "language": "en", "pack_id": null, "prompt_seed": "af636849077fb5c0", "created_at": "2026-10-14T13:05:08.696958", "created_by": null, "source": "generated", "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikachu", "source_excerpt": "Chū (チュー) imitates the squeak of a mouse, which fits its rodent-like form.", "usage_count": 0, "user_ratings": {}, "review_status": "approved", "reviewed_by": "machine:gates-v1", "reviewed_at": "2026-10-14T11:09:26.865883Z", "review_notes": null, "quality_ratings": null, "generation_metadata": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "provider": "anthropic", "prompt_version": "direct_v1", "pipeline": null, "generation_flow": "fun-redesign-72", "generation_temperature": null, "critique_model": null, "critique_score": null, "reasoning_pattern": "name_origin", "fact_ids": [], "extra": {"stage": "initial_generation", "escape_hatch": true, "gen_craft_guards": true, "why_interesting": "The world's most famous Pokémon has a name that is pure sound effect. The player can work out the animal from Pikachu's design.", "topicality": {"tier": "none", "event_date": null, "rationale": "General pop-culture etymology."}, "factcheck_tier": "web", "verified": true, "verification_score": 0.9, "verification_notes": "Wikipedia confirms 'chū' imitates a mouse's squeak, so the claimed answer holds."}, "created_at": null}, "cost_cents": null, "media_url": null, "image_subtype": null, "media_duration_seconds": null, "explanation": "'Chu' is the Japanese 'squeak' of a mouse, and Pikachu is officially the Mouse Pokémon. Its name is essentially 'sparkle-squeak'.", "embedding_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "embedding_dim": 1536, "subtopic": null, "answer_key": null, "embedding_qa_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "expires_at": null, "freshness_tag": null, "boost_until": null}, {"id": "0499b766-3716-4e09-ba77-12c8fbff4c07", "question": "In the summer of 2014, celebrities worldwide tipped buckets of ice water over their heads in a viral challenge. It raised money for research into which disease?", "type": "text", "possible_answers": null, "correct_answer": "ALS", "alternative_answers": ["Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis", "Motor neurone disease", "Lou Gehrig's disease", "MND"], "headline_answer": null, "topic": "Viral pop culture", "category": "entertainment", "difficulty": "medium", "tags": [], "language_dependent": false, "age_appropriate": "all", "language": "en", "pack_id": null, "prompt_seed": "af636849077fb5c0", "created_at": "2026-10-14T13:05:08.696993", "created_by": null, "source": "generated", "source_url": "https://als.net/ibc", "source_excerpt": "co-founded by two young men with ALS – Pat Quinn, diagnosed with ALS at age 30 and Pete Frates, diagnosed with ALS at age 27.", "usage_count": 0, "user_ratings": {}, "review_status": "approved", "reviewed_by": "machine:gates-v1", "reviewed_at": "2026-10-14T11:09:26.865883Z", "review_notes": null, "quality_ratings": null, "generation_metadata": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "provider": "anthropic", "prompt_version": "direct_v1", "pipeline": null, "generation_flow": "fun-redesign-72", "generation_temperature": null, "critique_model": null, "critique_score": null, "reasoning_pattern": "viral_cause", "fact_ids": [], "extra": {"stage": "initial_generation", "escape_hatch": true, "gen_craft_guards": true, "why_interesting": "Everyone remembers the soaked celebrities, but far fewer recall what the stunt was for. The money it raised went on to fund real research breakthroughs.", "topicality": {"tier": "none", "event_date": null, "rationale": "Viral moment from 2014, no longer fresh."}, "factcheck_tier": "web", "verified": true, "verification_score": 0.9, "verification_notes": "The 2014 Ice Bucket Challenge raised funds for ALS research, per the ALS Association (https://als.net/ibc); no newer event changes this."}, "created_at": null}, "cost_cents": null, "media_url": null, "image_subtype": null, "media_duration_seconds": null, "explanation": "The challenge raised well over 100 million dollars for ALS, also called motor neurone disease. That money helped fund the discovery of a new gene linked to the illness.", "embedding_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "embedding_dim": 1536, "subtopic": null, "answer_key": null, "embedding_qa_model": "text-embedding-3-small", "expires_at": null, "freshness_tag": null, "boost_until": null}, {"id": "96548723-c318-4c66-8318-2cc4eecdbf2d", "question": "Netflix's crime series The Gentlemen returned for a second season in 2026. In the first season, a young army officer inherits his father's grand English country estate, then discovers what illegal business running underneath it?", "type": "text", "possible_answers": null, "correct_answer": "A cannabis farm", "alternative_answers": ["Cannabis", "Weed farm", "Marijuana farm", "Marijuana growing", "Drug farm", "Cannabis empire"], "headline_answer": null, "topic": "TV & streaming", "category": "entertainment", "difficulty": "medium", "tags": [], "language_dependent": false, "age_appropriate": "all", "language": "en", "pack_id": null, "prompt_seed": "af636849077fb5c0", "created_at": "2026-10-14T13:07:44.668619", "created_by": null, "source": "generated", "source_url": "https://www.sortiraparis.com/en/what-to-do-in-paris/cinema-series/articles/309471-the-gentlemen-season-2-is-now-available-on-netflix-with-season-3-already-confirmed", "source_excerpt": "Eddie Horniman, played by Theo James, inherits his family's sprawling estate following his father's passing. He soon uncovers that these lands hide a cannabis empire.", "usage_count": 0, "user_ratings": {}, "review_status": "approved", "reviewed_by": "machine:gates-v1", "reviewed_at": "2026-10-14T11:09:26.865883Z", "review_notes": null, "quality_ratings": null, "generation_metadata": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "provider": "anthropic", "prompt_version": "direct_v1", "pipeline": null, "generation_flow": "fun-redesign-72", "generation_temperature": null, "critique_model": null, "critique_score": null, "reasoning_pattern": "hidden_secret_reveal", "fact_ids": [], "extra": 